# Reference list of hub settings. Copy to hub/.env (gitignored) and the hub loads it at startup.
# The hub, `pnpm eval`, and the hub tests all read this file. Variables already set in your shell win over the file. Every value has a safe default. Nothing here is a secret.

# HTTP listener. 0.0.0.0 serves the whole local network.
HUB_HOST=0.0.0.0
HUB_PORT=8787

# Matcher: keyword (default), laya, or hybrid (keyword first, Laya only when keyword is not sure).
# laya and hybrid need LAYA_PASS_MARK and LAYA_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD. Both are team decisions, set before the labeled test.
HUB_MATCHER=keyword
# LAYA_PASS_MARK=
# LAYA_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD=

# Laya weights. The hub downloads nothing: laya and hybrid need LAYA_MODEL_DIR, a local bundle. The bundle must be the ONNX export
# (laya.onnx, laya.onnx.data, laya_config.json, tokenizer/). The raw convaiinnovations checkpoint will not load. See README, "Laya bundle".
LAYA_CACHE=.laya-cache
# LAYA_MODEL_DIR=

# Where Laya runs: webgpu (default, about 0.1 s per question) or cpu (about 1 s). webgpu falls back to cpu by itself if it fails.
# LAYA_DEVICE=webgpu

# If Laya fails to load, keep answering with keyword matching. "keyword" is the default.
HUB_LAYA_FALLBACK=keyword

# Explainer: off (default) or gemma. gemma runs a local Gemma 4 E2B model.
# If it fails to load, the hub keeps answering with the matched notes only.
HUB_EXPLAINER=off
# Runtime: llamacpp (default, GPU, about 1 to 2 s per answer) or onnx (CPU, 12 s to a minute).
# GEMMA_RUNTIME=llamacpp
# llamacpp needs the GGUF file. Download once from hub/:
#   npx node-llama-cpp pull --dir E:/models/gemma-4-E2B-it-GGUF hf:ggml-org/gemma-4-E2B-it-GGUF:Q4_0
# GEMMA_GGUF=E:/models/gemma-4-E2B-it-GGUF/hf_ggml-org_gemma-4-E2B-it.Q4_0.gguf
# onnx needs the ONNX folder. q4f16 on cpu is its tested route. DirectML on the GPU gave empty output.
# GEMMA_MODEL_DIR=E:/models/gemma-4-E2B-it-ONNX
# GEMMA_DTYPE=q4f16
# GEMMA_DEVICE=cpu
# Word limit in the prompt. The phone keeps 150; the hub asks for 130 so a best guess has room for its steps.
# GEMMA_MAX_WORDS=130
# Answers are cut at this many tokens.
# GEMMA_MAX_NEW_TOKENS=320

# Photo reading: off (default) or on. With on, the hub reads a photo the farmer attaches: the local Gemma 4 ONNX
# vision model writes down the signs it sees (about 30 s on the CPU), and matching reads them with the farmer's words.
# Needs GEMMA_MODEL_DIR (the ONNX folder), whichever GEMMA_RUNTIME writes the explanation. Uses GEMMA_DTYPE and GEMMA_DEVICE.
# HUB_VISION=off
# VISION_MAX_NEW_TOKENS=128

# Speech reading: off (default) or on. With on, the farmer can speak instead of type: the phone records up to 30 s and the
# local Gemma 4 ONNX audio model writes it down (about 25 s on the CPU) into the question box. It shares one model with HUB_VISION.
# Needs GEMMA_MODEL_DIR. Browsers allow the microphone only on localhost or https, so phones need the hub's https address (see README, Pending decisions).
# HUB_VOICE=off
# VOICE_MAX_NEW_TOKENS=192
# Weight variant for photo and speech reading. Defaults to GEMMA_DTYPE (q4f16, about 3 GB). quantized (8-bit, about 6 GB) wrote Tagalog
# and Cebuano speech more exactly on the 4 real clips tried (character errors 3.2% and 8.5%, against 7.9% and 10.9%), but loading it failed
# with "bad allocation" when under 7 GB of memory was free. Use it on a hub machine with plenty of free memory.
# MULTIMODAL_DTYPE=q4f16
# AUDIO_DTYPE is for the audio encoder alone. Changing only that part helped little in the one clip tried (see README, Voice).
# AUDIO_DTYPE=

# Android app's on-phone Gemma 4 E2B (LiteRT-LM). The hub only serves this file at /models/litert/gemma-4-E2B-it.litertlm and the
# app downloads it once over Wi-Fi (about 2.4 GB, resumable). Unset, /health reports phoneLitert "off" and the app cannot get the model.
# PHONE_LITERT_FILE=E:/models/gemma-4-E2B-it-litert-lm/gemma-4-E2B-it.litertlm

# Pilot logs, one JSON line per event. Keep off git.
HUB_LOG_DIR=logs
